        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{y}{4}-\\dfrac{x}{3}=1$ and $\\dfrac{x}{2}+\\dfrac{y}{5}=10$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=12,\\ y=20$",
          "worked": "Multiplying the first equation by $12$ and the second by $10$ gives:\n$$\\begin{align*}-4x+3y&=12\\\\5x+2y&=100\\end{align*}$$\nTwice the first and three times the second give:\n$$\\begin{align*}-8x+6y&=24\\\\15x+6y&=300\\end{align*}$$\nSubtracting:\n$$\\begin{align*}23x&=276\\\\x&=12\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting:\n$$\\begin{align*}5(12)+2y&=100\\\\2y&=40\\\\y&=20\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$4x+\\dfrac{y-2}{3}=12$ and $3y-\\dfrac{x-3}{5}=6$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=3,\\ y=2$",
          "worked": "Multiplying the first equation by $3$ and the second by $5$:\n$$\\begin{align*}12x+y-2&=36\\\\12x+y&=38\\\\15y-x+3&=30\\\\-x+15y&=27\\end{align*}$$\nFifteen times $12x+y=38$ gives $180x+15y=570$. Subtracting $-x+15y=27$:\n$$\\begin{align*}181x&=543\\\\x&=3\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting:\n$$\\begin{align*}12(3)+y&=38\\\\y&=2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      }
    ]
